The Tamsui Plot was a political dispute within the Zong colony of [[Colonial Zong]] in 1543, in which the colonial administration at [[Tamsui]] refused to obey the orders of the colonial government at [[Anping]]. It was not an armed confrontation. It consisted of a sustained refusal of authority, conducted through correspondence, and it ended with the division of the colony into the [[Sinchew]] and [[Louyang]] territories.

The episode is significant as the origin of the two colonies. The Sinchew and Louyang territories, and the modern state of [[Sinchew]] that descends from one of them, owe their existence to the administrative division that resolved this dispute. It was long described as the Tamsui Insurgency, a term implying armed resistance; later scholarship has rejected that description, the officials at Tamsui having neither raised forces nor seized property.

== Background ==

''See also: [[Colonial Zong]]''

The Zong colonial territory on the western coast of [[Lurandia]] was governed from [[Anping]] from 1474. The settlement at Tamsui was founded in 1538 as a colonial city within that territory, on the northwestern coast.

The two settlements developed along different lines. Anping was the older foundation, with an established plantation economy oriented toward the south-western districts, a large garrison, and a direct relationship with the court in [[Zong]]. Tamsui was a newer settlement on a frontier, populated by penal transportees and by settlers drawn from the merchant and plantation classes of the older colony. Its economy was oriented toward the sea trade and toward the interior of the northern coast, which the [[First Mahik War]] had brought under Zong control in 1542.

The grievance that produced the plot was administrative rather than economic. The government at Anping directed the colony's investment, military support and appointments toward the districts around itself, and the northwestern settlement received comparatively little of each.

The colony consisted of two separated holdings, and the voyage between them was long. Despatches issued at Anping took months to reach Tamsui, so that the northwestern settlement was governed at a delay of a season or more and instructions frequently arrived after the circumstances that had prompted them had changed.


The dispute turned on the terms on which the northwestern settlement was supplied. Under Okheng the territory had traded directly, receiving goods and people from Zong without the intervention of any other authority. On its acquisition by the colony in 1535 the territory became part of a single colonial administration, and all goods and people bound for Tamsui were thereafter required to pass through [[Anping]] first. The northwestern settlement received what was left after the older colony had taken its share.

The arrangement had been tolerable while the [[First Mahik War]] was being fought. Between 1538 and 1542 the territory was the base for the Zong campaign on the northern coast and was supplied accordingly: troops, provisions and material passed through it in quantity, and the settlement was better provisioned in those four years than at any time before or after.

The war ended on 7 April 1542, and the flow of supplies ended with it. The settlement's requests for goods, for stores and for the means to maintain the works it had built during the war were answered slowly or not at all. Its petitions for permission to import directly from Zong were refused, and the requirement that all cargo pass through Anping was enforced. The correspondence of the period records repeated applications from Tamsui and repeated delays at Anping.

The hostility was not merely administrative. A body of opinion at Anping held that the northern territory was a waste of the colony's resources, that the war had been fought for a harbour that did not return what it cost, and that the settlement's difficulties were of its own making. Those who held that view were in a position to act upon it, and the evidence of the papers is that shipments were slowed and applications obstructed beyond what the ordinary business of administration required.

== Course of events ==

From about 1542 the Tamsui administration ceased to observe the restriction on direct trade. Goods were obtained from Zong by other means, and the settlement was supplied without reference to Anping.

The method was the employment of private merchants. Houses trading out of Zong were engaged to carry cargo to Tamsui directly, bypassing the requirement that it pass through the older colony. The traffic was conducted over the course of about a year and was known to the settlement's merchant houses, which participated in it.

The arrangement was administered at Tamsui by Tân Kéng-seng (陳景生), the senior colonial official of the settlement, acting with the support of its merchant houses and plantation owners and upon the authority of the governor. He conducted the correspondence with Anping, arranged the engagement of the private carriers, and it was he who answered the older colony's requisitions. The administration of the settlement at Tamsui was in his hands, and the refusals of 1542 and 1543 were carried out by him.

The arrangement was discovered at Anping by 11 August 1543. On that date the administration at Anping had evidence that Tamsui had been trading directly with Zong through private merchants.


=== Envoy ===

[[Chiu Khéng-gio̍k]], who had governed the territory since 1527 and had served as its provisional administrator since 1535, concluded on the discovery of the traffic that his position was lost. On 4 September 1543 he despatched an envoy from Tamsui to the mainland, sailing in secret and without the knowledge of the administration at Anping.

The envoy's instructions were to petition the court at [[Huwei]] directly. The petition sought not the separation of the territory from the colony but the governance of it on terms of its own, without the requirement that its trade pass through Anping. It was carried by a vessel which reached Zong in due course, the passage from Tamsui to Huwei being some 10,711 km and a voyage of four to five months, and it therefore arrived early in 1544.

=== Force ===

Anping had already acted. A force was despatched from the older colony to depose Chiu Khéng-gio̍k and place him under arrest, and on 20 November 1543 it arrested him at Tamsui, before the envoy's petition had been heard at the court. He remained in custody thereafter until the edict of 1544 took effect.

The administration at Anping reported the matter to Zong and characterised what had happened at Tamsui as an attempt by its administrator to restore his own polity — to reconstitute the territory of Okheng as a jurisdiction independent of the colony. The characterisation was not one the papers of the settlement support, and it became the ground upon which the colony's case against its northwestern territory was argued.

=== Investigation ===

The petition reached the court before or at about the same time as the report from Anping. The court, receiving two irreconcilable accounts of the same events, did not act upon either. It despatched its own investigators to the colony to determine the facts.

The inquiry established that no force had been raised at Tamsui and no territory had been seized; that the officers of the settlement had neither deposed nor resisted the governor; and that the substance of the dispute was the terms on which the settlement was supplied. The [[Societal Assembly]] had before it, as it had had in the earlier correspondence, a breakdown of authority in the colony rather than a rebellion.

== Resolution ==

The matter came before the [[Societal Assembly]] of Zong. Confronted with a colony whose two principal settlements would not cooperate, the assembly resolved the dispute by separating them.

The division was made by royal edict, deliberated in the assembly and approved by [[Tiancheng of Zong|Emperor Tiancheng]]. The edict was issued at [[Huwei]] on 1 June 1544. It did not take effect in the colony until 11 November of that year, the passage from Tamsui to Huwei being some 10,711 km and a voyage of four to five months; the colony was therefore divided in law from June and in fact from November.

When the edict took effect at Tamsui on 11 November 1544, Chiu Khéng-gio̍k was released, having been in custody for about a year. He was not tried, and no penalty is recorded against him; the office of provisional administrator was dissolved, and a replacement administrator was sent from Anping to take charge of the northwestern territory under the new arrangement.

The colonial territory was divided along the [[Qingjiang]] river, which empties into the sea at the Hook of Tianhe. The territory northwest of the river, centred on Tamsui, was constituted as the [[Sinchew]] colony. The territory southeast of the river, centred on Anping, remained the [[Louyang]] colony.

The division was administrative and territorial rather than disciplinary. No punitive action was taken against the officials at Tamsui, and the grievances that had prompted the refusal were addressed by the reorganization itself: the northwestern settlement received its own governor, its own administration, and its own direct relationship with the court.

The boundary was drawn with reference to the geography of the colony rather than to the dispute. The Sinchew territory comprised the lands north of the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]] state; the Louyang territory comprised the lands to its south.

== Aftermath ==

Each colony was thereafter governed by its own governor and reported directly to the court. Louyang remained the wealthier and more established of the two, with its plantation economy and its closer commercial ties to Zong, while Sinchew developed as a frontier society.

The division did not end the administrative tensions it had resolved. The two colonies retained distinct interests and distinct relationships with the court, and the political divergence between them widened over the following century.

